  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ADVANCING ELEPHANT CONSERVATION AND WILDLIFE MIGRATORY ROUTES IN KENYA THROUGH STRENGTHENING COLLABORATION AND INCREASING LANDSCAPE CONNECTIVITY IN LAIKIPIA, KENYA.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The purpose of this project is to support an elephant conservation effort on private and community conservancy land in Laikipia County by strengthening conservancy association coordination and organizational capacity, elephant protection, biological monitoring, and community engagement. As part of the Laikipia-Samburu Ecosystem savanna elephant stronghold, this landscape is home to Kenyas second largest elephant population, which includes approximately 7,000 individuals. This project is intended to conserve African savanna elephants and their habitat by addressing the impacts of human-elephant conflict (HEC), poaching, and habitat fragmentation. Specific activities include: (1) increasing staff and operational support to conservancy members; (2) creating a Corridor Management Committee and conducting stakeholder mapping and corridor consensus building with communities; (3) establishing and fencing an elephant corridor and conducting wildlife monitoring; and (4) providing basic field gear (boots, binoculars etc.) for ranger teams conducting anti-poaching patrols and monitoring the movements of elephants across the corridor (cameras, phones etc.)</narrative>
  </description>
